<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lapulapu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">16th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Lapu-Lapu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lapulapu was a chief of Mactan, an island off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cebu">Cebu</a> in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, who refused to submit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. On 27 April 1521 his warriors defeated a Spanish landing party at the Battle of Mactan and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-magellan">Ferdinand Magellan</a>. He is honoured in the Philippines as the first native leader to resist European colonisation, and a city on Mactan bears his name.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Very little is reliably known about Lapulapu. The only primary source that names him is the account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-pigafetta">Antonio Pigafetta</a>, who recorded the name as Çilapulapu and noted two datus of Mactan, Zula and Lapulapu. The historian Resil B. Mojares observes that no European who left a record of Magellan's voyage saw him or heard him speak. Cebuano oral tradition holds that his father was a datu named Mangal. The Aginid, a book published in 1952 by Jovito Abellana, makes him a settler from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>, but most Filipino historians regard it as a hoax or as imaginative fiction. The evidence points to a Visayan follower of the indigenous anito beliefs, not a Muslim, as some later traditions claim.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-magellan">Ferdinand Magellan</a> reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> in Spanish service, Zula gave tribute to the Spanish king, but Lapulapu refused. On 27 April 1521 Magellan came to Mactan with about 60 Spaniards and boats of warriors from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajah-humabon">Rajah Humabon</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cebu">Cebu</a>. Rocks and reefs kept his ships off shore, so Magellan and 49 heavily armoured men waded in, telling Humabon's warriors to stay in their boats. According to Pigafetta, they faced about 1,500 warriors. Magellan was wounded in the leg by a poisoned arrow, was recognised as the captain and was overwhelmed and killed.</p><p>The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-henry-scott">William Henry Scott</a> suggests that Magellan wrongly assumed that Humabon, as king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cebu">Cebu</a>, ruled Mactan too, when Visayan societies were loose federations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a>. Mactan's position let its chiefs intercept ships entering Cebu's harbour, so Lapulapu may have been the more powerful. Humabon was married to Lapulapu's niece. The battle left the expedition with too few men to crew three ships, and it abandoned the Concepción. Spanish expansion into the archipelago was delayed until the expedition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-lopez-de-legazpi">Miguel López de Legazpi</a> in 1565.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Lapulapu is regarded, retroactively, as the first Filipino hero. A shrine was built on Mactan in 1969, the town of Opon became Lapu-Lapu City, and his face appeared on the 1-centavo coin from 1967 to 1994. In 2021 an executive order fixed the spelling Lapulapu, without a hyphen, following Pigafetta's original. Local legend says he never died but was turned into stone and still guards the sea of Mactan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lapulapu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Magellan died fighting the forces of the chief <b>Lapulapu</b> in the Philippines.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-27">27 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Rajah Humabon and hundreds of his people were baptised, but the alliance broke down after <b>Lapulapu</b> of Mactan killed Magellan in battle on 27 April 1521.</blockquote><a href="/state/rajahnate-of-cebu">Rajahnate of Cebu</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mactan, in what is now <a href="/countries/philippines">the Philippines</a>. Died in Mactan, in what is now <a href="/countries/philippines">the Philippines</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=10.3,123.97&amp;year=1564" aria-label="Mactan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Lapulapu"><image href="/maps/globes/g12000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5334 4124h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5334 4124h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=10.3,123.97&amp;year=1564">What was here in 1564? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
